B2 — DISCHARGED. The header names a tree the command actually ran in.

  Round 3's B2: all twelve logs read `# tree:
  tree:b7edeb01426089311aa5ad612fefa1ffbc702f6c`, HEAD's source tree, in which
  the file nine of them said could not be found is 244 lines long. The cause
  was in red, not in builder-b's hands: sourceTreeHash hashed the repo red was
  invoked from while runCapture ran the command from the caller's cwd.

  ae22e88 adds `red --cwd`: the command runs there and the header names the
  git repo containing that directory, walking up via `git rev-parse
  --show-toplevel` and recording "none" when there is none. Without --cwd the
  default derivation now follows the caller's cwd rather than assuming the
  officina's parent repo — so the defect is fixed at the root, not only on the
  path W-021 took.

  All twelve logs now read `# tree: dirty:91e92caf50f48680b075cc16b709bb5d5be
  4e244`, the stubbed worktree. No log any longer certifies a tree that would
  not produce the recorded failure. Round 3's stated bar for W-021 was "run
  red with its cwd inside the tree under test, or accept unknown in the
  header". The first of those is what happened.


