CARRIED FORWARD, UNCHANGED AND NON-BLOCKING

  F2  `exit: 00` reads as a red. evidence.ts:191 tests `exit === "0"` as a
      string; `Number(exit) === 0` is the one-character fix. Re-measured above
      and still live. It needs a hand-forged log to exploit — `bisellium red`
      cannot emit `00` — and no log in either officina has that shape.
  F3  opus.untracked is blind to a gitignored opus: `git ls-files --others
      --exclude-standard` omits ignored files. Spec-compliant; a scope
      question for the rule's next revision.
  F5  Behaviour 12's assertion over `studio` relaxed to "no blocking path.*"
      after the level drop. Correct handling, documented in P-004.
  F6  The insurance clause was applied to both path.* rules though only
      path.escapes.officina ever fired. Brief-compliant; recorded so P-004's
      resolution can re-promote them separately.

  F4 remains closed (2412500).


NEW, ADVISORY, FOR THE RETRO

  A5  The two skeletons are asymmetric. evidence.ts over-fires, so the
      silence-only assertions in behaviours 3, 4 and 5 go red. paths.ts is a
      no-op, so the silence-only assertions in 10 and 11 stay green — including
      behaviour 11's central clause, "a path that merely looks like an escape
      but resolves inside passes, proving the relation not a string prefix",
      which is the whole reason D-008 came back a third time. An over-firing
      paths.ts skeleton, symmetric with the evidence.ts one, would have redded
      it. This is not blocking: behaviour 11 is shown failing, and the
      behaviour is the granularity both lex §2 and the store work at. But the
      one assertion that would catch a startsWith regression is the one with no
      red behind it, and that is worth saying out loud.

  A6  The reds are reconstructions and the store cannot say so. The stubbed
      worktree was removed after recording and the skeleton sources were never
      committed, so dirty:91e92ca… names a tree that exists nowhere. The header
      is now honest — it no longer certifies a tree that contradicts the body —
      but a cold reader still cannot reproduce or even identify what the
      failure was produced against. W-020's log format has no field for
      "reconstructed against skeleton X at commit Y". That is the remaining
      half of B2 and it belongs to W-020 or a petitio, not here.

  A7  For the retro, restating round 3's cheapest lesson now that it has been
      answered by hand rather than by a rule: the store's shape is checkable
      and is not checked. A rule that blocks when two logs under one opus have
      byte-identical bodies would have caught round 3's B1 without a reviewer.
      Round 4 shows the defect is fixable; it does not show it is prevented.
      L-001/L-008/L-017's class, fifth appearance.


